1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,439
Code rots.

2
00:00:01,739 --> 00:00:05,920
Every change can open a hole: a bug, a
missing test, stale docs.

3
00:00:06,970 --> 00:00:09,018
So symcc sends out scouts.

4
00:00:09,318 --> 00:00:12,475
They wake on timers, read the code, and
hunt for problems.

5
00:00:12,775 --> 00:00:17,106
One found that the answer, no rush, give
it another hour, would stop a worker.

6
00:00:17,406 --> 00:00:19,113
Each finding becomes a task.

7
00:00:20,163 --> 00:00:21,251
You can ask too.

8
00:00:21,551 --> 00:00:24,068
Tell the lead in your chat, and it writes
a task.

9
00:00:24,368 --> 00:00:26,096
Both kinds meet on one board.

10
00:00:27,146 --> 00:00:28,533
Then the pipeline runs.

11
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:31,179
A worker builds the task in its own copy.

12
00:00:31,479 --> 00:00:33,378
Tests must pass before it merges.

13
00:00:33,678 --> 00:00:36,153
If they fail, it goes back for another
try.

14
00:00:37,203 --> 00:00:38,568
Every task is a page.

15
00:00:38,868 --> 00:00:41,407
What and why, which files, and where it
came from.

16
00:00:41,707 --> 00:00:44,331
Its tries, the report, the time and
tokens.

17
00:00:45,381 --> 00:00:46,533
The lead judges.

18
00:00:46,833 --> 00:00:48,518
Bugs and safety go first.

19
00:00:48,818 --> 00:00:51,613
When two tasks touch the same files, one
waits.

20
00:00:51,913 --> 00:00:56,435
Every commit carries its task number, so
each change traces back to its request.

21
00:00:57,485 --> 00:00:58,659
And the holes close.

22
00:00:58,959 --> 00:01:02,358
In this one project, two hundred seventy
tasks are done,

23
00:01:02,358 --> 00:01:04,057
sixty three found by scouts.

24
00:01:04,357 --> 00:01:06,256
The product gets better every day.

25
00:01:07,306 --> 00:01:08,351
symcc.

26
00:01:08,651 --> 00:01:10,251
Many agents, one board.
